+### Parallel execution with communication
+
+Set `communication: true` on a parallel call. Give tasks explicit `id` values when meaningful names help; omitted IDs become `worker-1`, `worker-2`, and so on.
+
+```json
+{
+  "tasks": [
+    { "id": "api", "agent": "worker", "task": "Inspect the API constraints and message the implementer." },
+    { "id": "implementer", "agent": "worker", "task": "Implement the change and use findings from api." },
+    { "id": "reviewer", "agent": "reviewer", "task": "Review the implementation and broadcast blockers." }
+  ],
+  "communication": true
+}
+```
+
+Communication adds these tools to each worker:
+
+| Tool | Arguments | Result |
+|------|-----------|--------|
+| `team_list` | `{}` | This worker plus every teammate's ID, agent definition, and `pending`, `running`, or `finished` status |
+| `team_send` | `{ recipient, message }` | Acceptance for one running teammate, or an explicit error |
+| `team_broadcast` | `{ message }` | Acceptance per running teammate, including partial-failure details |
+
+Example worker calls:
+
+```json
+{ "recipient": "implementer", "message": "The interface requires AbortSignal to remain optional." }
+```
+
+```json
+{ "message": "Do not use the legacy endpoint; its response omits tool IDs." }
+```
+
+Messages are asynchronous. A successful result means the recipient accepted the message for delivery, not that it replied. Accepted messages enter the recipient's context after its current model response and tool calls, before its next model call. If the worker was about to finish, the accepted message causes another model turn. Running tools are not interrupted.
+
+Only currently running teammates can receive messages, and a worker cannot message itself. A direct send to a pending, finished, or unknown worker fails. Broadcasts exclude the sender and report every recipient that did not accept the message. Message text is limited to 64 KiB measured as UTF-8 bytes; oversized and malformed requests fail instead of being truncated.
+
+Each dispatch uses an ephemeral loopback broker with a random credential per worker. Worker IDs are unique within that dispatch, so multiple tasks may use the same agent definition. The broker closes on completion or cancellation; separate and later dispatches cannot access its messages. Communication does not provide cross-session messaging, crash recovery, nested teams, or persistent shared memory.
+
